## Purpose

A project's front door is only finished when a stranger can walk through it. Writing the files is the easy part; the test is whether someone who has never heard of the project can read them and say what it is and how they'd start helping, with no one around to explain. That holds for an open-source project, an internal tool at work, or a library you publish: the people who most need the docs are the ones who can't ask you.

Today the front door gets built, and it gets built the way any change to the project gets made: through **pull requests**, under the git standards you wrote. The process documents itself.

The one thing taught today is **issue and pull request templates**: where the files go in your repository, the two formats an issue template can take, and real projects' versions to compare. Then you build. Not everything fits in one class, so the session is ordered by what the room is good for. The items where a standard already exists (license text, a code of conduct, issue templates) get **finished** here, where help is close. The items only you can write (CONTRIBUTING, your communication norms) get **started**, far enough for someone to read. And then someone does: your review partner reads your README and CONTRIBUTING cold, the one test an author can't run on their own docs. What they find, you fix, today or by the deadline.

---

## Learning objectives

By the end of this session, you'll be able to:

1. **Set up issue and pull request templates**: put the files where GitHub reads them, choose between a Markdown template and an issue form, and build a pull request template from your git standards.
2. **Adapt standard artifacts** (license text, the Contributor Covenant, issue and pull request templates) to your project rather than copying them blindly or writing your own from scratch, and ship them through pull requests.
3. **Grow a CONTRIBUTING guide from your git standards**, leading with a welcome and a first step before any policy.
4. **Read another project's front door as a stranger** and say, in writing, what it is and how you'd start helping, and **act on that kind of read** of your own.
5. **Write an honest status** for a project that doesn't run yet: a setup placeholder rather than a quickstart that fails.
6. **Seed your issue tracker** with well-formed issues that follow your own templates.

