## What we'll do together

**Two rules for the day.** *Adapt, don't invent,* wherever a standard exists: the exact license text, the [Contributor Covenant](https://www.contributor-covenant.org/version/3/0/), the structure of an issue form. Experts wrote those; your job is to fill in your blanks. *Write, don't copy,* wherever your project is the content: README, CONTRIBUTING, communication norms. A stranger can tell a template from a project that knows what it is.

**A worked example.** [Prop Loft](https://github.com/prop-loft/prop-loft/tree/session-16), the course's sample project, has a finished front door at this point in the semester: a CONTRIBUTING guide that opens with a welcome, a code of conduct with only its blanks filled in, issue forms, a pull request template, communication norms, and seed issues. Read it for shape. Your content has to be yours.

**Templates, taught (15 min).** A template turns your reporting norms into the default: whatever it asks for is what you'll get, in every issue and every pull request.

- **Where the files go.** Everything lives in a `.github/` folder at the root of your repository: issue templates in `.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/` (each file becomes one choice when someone clicks **New issue**), an optional `config.yml` beside them that controls that chooser, and `.github/pull_request_template.md`, which pre-fills every new pull request's description. None of it works until it's merged to your default branch.
- **Two formats for issue templates.** A **Markdown template** (`.md`) pre-fills one text box with your headings; a reporter can delete anything, and it takes minutes to write. An **issue form** (`.yml`) gives separate fields, and a field marked `required: true` can't be skipped. Both are fine, and you can mix them.
- **The pull request template** is Markdown only. Its top half is the description you've been writing by hand (the problem, the approach, where to look, how you tested); its bottom half is your git standards' definition of done, as a checklist the author ticks before asking for review.
- **Three real projects**, to read for what they ask: a BYU course-tools project that kept GitHub's generated starter, a popular Python library whose Markdown templates do a lot, and Prop Loft's forms.

The <course-link type="page" id="pg-issue-pr-templates">Issue and Pull Request Templates</course-link> page has all of it, with copy-ready skeletons and links to the three examples. Build from it, in class or after.

**Finish the fast items (15 min).** Each in its own small pull request:

- **README:** your first sentence, rewritten; a status line; how to contribute; a license line. Instead of a quickstart, an honest *not runnable yet* that points to the setup placeholder.
- **Setup placeholder** (`docs/setup.md`): the stack your design commits to, a line saying setup instructions arrive with the first runnable build, and a link to the issue that tracks writing them. File that issue now; it's your first seed issue.
- **LICENSE:** the exact text from [choosealicense.com](https://choosealicense.com/licenses/), changed only where the license asks you to fill something in. MIT and BSD want a year and copyright holder. Apache-2.0 and the GPL have nothing to fill in; Apache's `[yyyy] [name of copyright owner]` line is a template for file headers and stays as written.
- **Code of conduct:** the Contributor Covenant, through GitHub's *Add file → CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md* template. Read the enforcement section so you know what you've promised. The reporting contact is public, and it has to work: an address you'll check and are willing to publish (it needn't be your personal one), how soon reports get read, and where to go if the report is about *you*. On a one-person project, that's [GitHub's reporting tools](https://docs.github.com/en/communities/maintaining-your-safety-on-github/reporting-abuse-or-spam) or someone else who can act.
- **Templates and labels:** create your [labels](https://docs.github.com/en/issues/using-labels-and-milestones-to-track-work/managing-labels) first (`bug`, `enhancement`, `good first issue`, plus your project's own), because a form skips any label the repository doesn't have, without a warning. Then build last class's sketches from the templates page's skeletons: a bug template, a feature request, and a pull request template. File one test issue through each template to check. If templates don't finish in this block, finish them by the deadline.

**Start the write-it-yourself items (15 min).** **CONTRIBUTING**, grown from your git standards: a welcome and what to do first, then how to pick work, the pull request process, and review expectations, with policy last. **Communication norms** (`docs/communication-norms.md`): where discussion happens, response times you can actually keep ("within a week," not "24/7"), who maintains, and how decisions get made. *Working solo? That last question still needs an answer: when a contributor proposes something, how does it get decided, and where?* And since a response time is a promise, say how you'll keep it: how will you know which new issues are still waiting for an answer? A label your forms apply and you remove after reading, a *New* column on a project board, a fixed day you read everything new: any of these works. Pick one and name it.

**The stranger's read (15 min).** You and your review partner swap repositories. In a private, logged-out browser window, read their README and then their CONTRIBUTING, about five minutes each, without asking them anything. On the **Stranger's Read sheet**, write what the project is, how you'd start helping, and the one thing that most stopped you. Then hand the sheet back. *(With an odd number of people, a group of three rotates: each person reads one repository and is read by one.)*

**Act on it (8 min).** Read your sheet. If your partner's answer to *what is this?* isn't what you meant, your first sentence isn't done. Fix what you can now; file an issue for anything bigger, and say "from the stranger's read" in the pull request or issue, so the fix traces back to the read. Then start filing two or three real next-work issues through your own templates; finish them by the deadline if time runs out. Before there's code, most will be features or tasks; a task that fits neither form uses the closest one, or you add a task form. They're the start of next week's backlog.

